sangati apeksha

  -- apeksha: with-respect-to.
  -- the denominator variable in a derivative.
  -- d(x)/d(t): x is kramanusara, t is apeksha.
  --
  -- without apeksha, a kramanusara edge is incomplete —
  -- you know WHAT was differentiated but not WITH RESPECT TO WHAT.
  --
  -- in physics, apeksha is almost always time (second).
  -- in geometry it can be space (metre).
  -- in thermodynamics it can be temperature (kelvin) or volume.
  -- in general: any quantity can be the apeksha of any other.
  --
  -- apeksha is the denominator that gives kramanusara its unit:
  --   displacement -[kramanusara]-> velocity
  --   velocity -[apeksha]-> time
  --   unit(velocity) = unit(displacement) / unit(time) = metre / second

  "viveka-yukta kramanusara-sthita"
  "matra-janya sankhya-yukta"

  -- two kinds of apeksha — this distinction is fundamental:
  --
  -- sthira-apeksha: the reference itself does not move
  --   time is the canonical sthira-apeksha — it passes but does not itself accelerate
  --   world-frame (base of a robot arm) is sthira-apeksha for all joint positions
  --   kramanusara wrt sthira-apeksha = ordinary derivative d/dt
  --
  -- chala-apeksha: the reference itself is in motion
  --   in a kinematic chain: joint i's local frame is the apeksha for joint i+1's position
  --   but joint i's frame moves whenever joint i (or any ancestor) changes angle
  --   kramanusara wrt chala-apeksha = total derivative (includes transport term)
  --   this produces Coriolis and centripetal terms in equations of motion:
  --     d(v)/dt_world = d(v)/dt_local + ω × v   (transport theorem)
  --   where ω is the angular velocity of the moving frame itself
  --
  -- the distinction matters for torque computation:
  --   naive τ = I·α ignores the moving-frame coupling between joints
  --   correct τ requires Newton-Euler or Lagrangian dynamics with chala-apeksha terms
  --
  -- in the graph: sthira-apeksha edges are the current kramanusara edges on velocity etc.
  --   chala-apeksha edges connect joint frames in the kinematic chain

  -- two forms — sangati nodes in their own right:
  "sthira-apeksha-abheda chala-apeksha-abheda"
  "sambandha-yukta"

  shabda apeksha, wrt, with-respect-to / the-denominator-variable-of-a-derivative-fixed-sthira-or-moving-chala-determines-whether-transport-terms-arise

